(The development of the Airport Runway & Flight Manager) Tj
T* (follows a modular object-oriented workflow in C++.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (1. Requirement & Structure Design: System requirements were) Tj
T* (defined around core objects—Flights, Runways, Schedules, and) Tj
T* (Priority Queues. Classes were structured with private member) Tj
T* (variables and public member functions for controlled access.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (2. Flight & Fuel Monitoring Implementation: The system records) Tj
T* (details including Flight ID, Airline, Fuel Level, and Route. An) Tj
T* (automated condition evaluates fuel percentages; if fuel falls) Tj
T* (below the emergency threshold \(e.g., 20%\), the system) Tj
T* (automatically elevates the flight priority to EMERGENCY.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (3. Priority Queue & Runway Allocation: Flights are pushed into) Tj
T* (a dynamic priority queue sorted by emergency status, priority) Tj
T* (levels, and submission order. Available runways \(categorized as) Tj
T* (Landing, Takeoff, or Mixed\) are assigned based on suitability) Tj
T* (and status \(AVAILABLE, OCCUPIED, MAINTENANCE\).) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (4. File Processing & Report Generation: Data persistence is) Tj
T* (maintained by reading and writing binary data files \(flights.dat,) Tj
T* (runways.dat, schedule.dat, airport.dat\). Comprehensive reporting) Tj
T* (modules compile runway utilization metrics, emergency logs,) Tj
T* (and daily flight counts.) Tj
ET
